> You are trying to INSTALL right? (Some of the advice
> is telling you how to boot the hard disk)
> I have a Miata with similar hardware and I have
> RedHat 7.1 running.
> Clearly SRM can see your hardware else 'show dev'
> would not list it. Clearly 'dka0' is your CD drive.
> Lets do this one piece at a time...
>
> First make sure there are no funny args hidden in SRM
>
> >>>show boot*
>
> make sure the variables 'bootdef-dev', 'boot_osflags'
> are set to "" else SRM will use them
> Now type
>
> >>>boot dka0
>
> You should get
>
> aboot>
>
> IF YOU DO then this was read off the CDROM, now type an "L"
> for list
>
> aboot>l
>  This will list the options from aboot.conf, try each
> option in turn...
>
> aboot>0
> aboot>1
> aboot>2... etc
>
> If you get (as you said before)
> ================================
> aboot: loading compressed kernels/vmlinux.gz
>  unzip: crc error
> ================================
> Then the file 'kernels/vmlinux.gz' on the CDROM has a bad
> checksum. Try reading this file on another machine and
> do
> 'gunzip -l kernels/vmlinux.gz' to verify the file
>
> good file can be got from:
> ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/linux/7.1/en/os/alpha/kernels/
>
> There are two other things to do before RedHat installer
> will work properly. First zero the partition table
> >>>set os_type NT
> and reboot, use AlphaBIOS to delete existing partitions
> then switch back to SRM 'CMOS SETUP'->F6 set Console to SRM,
> reboot (now os_type should be Unix)
> Second you need to make a BSD label on the disk else
> after install SRM won't boot hard disk. See Installation notes
> paragraph 4
> ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/linux/7.1/en/os/alpha/RELEASE-NOTES
>
> I see you have more than one hard disk,
> DEC calls them 'dkc200', 'dkc400'
> RedHat calls them /dev/sda2, /dev/sda3
> so your flags will be 'root=/dev/sda2' or 'root=/dev/sda3'
>
